# OMN-13437 / OMN-13418 Phase 2a: un-forgeable prod-promotion trust anchor.
# This file is the canonical grant registry. Platform leads are the intended
# reviewers of any change to it, but this CODEOWNERS entry is ADVISORY, not
# enforced: `required_pull_request_reviews` is intentionally NOT enabled on
# `dev` (enabling it would block a platform-lead-authored grant PR from ever
# being approvable, since GitHub rejects self-approval of your own PR — see
# feedback_no_approval_required).
#
# CODEOWNERS review here is ADVISORY (see above). As of OMN-17157 it is no
# longer the ONLY defense against a self-approved prod-promotion grant: this
# repo now carries a MECHANICAL approver != requester control, in two halves,
# because the two authoring paths expose the requesting human in two
# different places.
#
#   1. Hand-authored PR that edits `grants/prod_promotion_grants.yaml`:
#      `validate-prod-promotion-grants`
#      (src/onex_change_control/scripts/validate_prod_promotion_grants.py),
#      run by the `validate-prod-promotion-grants` job in ci.yml under the
#      required CI Summary, refuses an entry NEW in the PR whose
#      `approved_by` is the PR author. Requester identity and the base
#      revision are both read from the GitHub API, never from the YAML.
#
#   2. The canonical bundle path (`stage-prod-promotion-grant.yml`): that
#      workflow opens its PR with the onexbot-occ-writer App token, so the
#      PR author is a bot and half 1 is VACUOUS there. It is instead refused
#      at DISPATCH time, where the requesting human is real — the workflow
#      rejects `inputs.approved_by == github.actor`.
#
# Both refuse with the reason string `self_granted`, the same token
# omninode_infra's promotion-time gate emits, so one grep finds every
# refusal of this condition. Neither half covers the other's path; do not
# remove one on the grounds that the other exists.
#
# OMN-14814 had removed the original OMN-14441 check on the premise that
# @OmniNode-ai/platform-leads "has exactly one member", making a second,
# different approver unsatisfiable. That premise no longer holds — the team
# has TWO members — which is what made restoring the control possible.
#
# OMN-14445 (CODEOWNERS review on `dev` is itself unenforced) remains OPEN
# and is NOT closed by the above: these are CI checks, which is mechanical
# enforcement that does not depend on review being required. What is still
# missing is enforced human review, not a mechanical control.
#
# The promotion-time control remains `approved_by != requested_by` in the
# omninode_infra dispatch gate
# (`omninode_infra/scripts/validate_prod_promotion_grant.py`, wired into
# `deploy-onex-prod.yml`). That compares against the DEPLOY DISPATCHER, an
# identity not knowable when the grant is authored, so this repo's checks
# narrow that window rather than replacing it.
#
# `check-platform-leads-review-tripwire` (OMN-14445) still runs. Its original
# premise — that OMN-14441's check was a complete defense only while
# platform-leads has exactly one member — is again partly live now that
# OMN-17157 has restored a self-approval check, but the tripwire still does
# not signal anything about that coverage: the restored checks are wired
# independently of team size, and OMN-14445 remains open to decide the
# CODEOWNERS-enforcement question the tripwire was standing in for.
grants/prod_promotion_grants.yaml @OmniNode-ai/platform-leads

# OMN-16702: PUBLIC-cluster staging-class namespace authorization anchor.
# Same ADVISORY status as the prod anchor above — `required_pull_request_reviews`
# is intentionally NOT enabled on `dev`, so this line does not mechanically
# block anything. Stated plainly so it is not mistaken for enforcement: the
# mechanical defenses on THIS anchor are scope-narrowing, not approver-
